  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday morning through next Sunday)\nIssued at 250 PM EDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nVery little change in the long term forecast for this afternoon. \nRidge continues to build in overhead through the week bringing \nmostly clear skies and some very warm temps to north and central \nGeorgia. Highs are still forecast to be 10-20 degrees above \naverage for highs and lows through Friday. Current forecast will \ndefinitely be close to record highs at a some of the climate \nsites, depending on the day and the site, and we very well could \nbreak some. This means highs in the mid to upper 80s and even a \nfew 90s. Overnight lows will be in the upper 50s and lower 60s. \nThis is above average, but not close to record high minimums which\nare generally in the 70s (think high dewpoint days/tropical \nevents in October). Air will be humid for October with dewpoints \nin the mid to upper 60s through Friday.\n\nLow over the 4 corners region is still progged to eject into the \nGreat Plains on Thursday and merge with trough digging into the \nsame area. Large trough spreads over the eastern CONUS driving \nearly season cold air down from the Arctic. Timing remains a \nlittle uncertain, but best current timing pushes the cold front in\nrapidly overnight Friday into Saturday morning. Much cooler air \ncomes in behind with highs on Saturday in the 60s and 70s. Sunday \nwill likely be the coolest night of the Fall so far, with lows in \nthe 40s and maybe a few 30s. 10th percentile NBM is showing things\nbeing right at the verge for frost advisory in parts of NE GA, so\nthat will bear some watching.",
